# mm.imu

A portable inertial measurement interface. It reports what the sensor measured
and what those numbers mean, and nothing else: orientation fusion, filtering,
gesture detection, and calibration are policy above this module, and controller
registers are detail below it.

Readings are signed counts in the sensor's own axes, exactly as mm.touch reports
coordinates in the panel's own space. scale says what a count is worth, so a
caller converts to whatever units it wants and no caller pays for units it does
not. Doing the conversion here would force floating point on every executable
that reaches this module, including bare-metal lanes with no hardware for it,
to produce one of the several unit systems callers actually want. The reference
drivers this interface was modelled on make that choice with a compile-time
switch, which is evidence that the choice belongs to the caller.

Temperature is hundredths of a degree Celsius for the same reason: it is the
sensor's precision expressed without a floating-point type.

The data-ready interrupt a sensor may offer is deliberately absent, as it is in
mm.touch: a caller polls read, and mm.mcu has no callback registration.

The platform-interface marker makes a selected board IMU provider part of an
executable only when its ordinary closure reaches mm.imu.
